    [], [#ch("BaO")], [0.262], [], [], [], [], [#ch("S")], [0.046],
    [], [#ch("CdO")], [0.031], [], [], [], [], [], [],
    [], [#ch("ZnO")], [0.465], [], [], [], [], [], [],
  )
]

In most cases the cadmium was introduced as cadmium oxide and the sulphur as zinc sulphide. These frits were the basic frits for yellow melted-in cadmium glazes. The milling formulae of the glazes had often 2 % of cadmium carbonate to support and stabilize the colour development.

A typical glaze composition for a melted-in type of yellow glaze was as follows:

#recipe[
  #align(center, table(
    columns: 2,
    stroke: none,
    align: (left, right),
    [basic frit containing #ch("Cd") and #ch("S")], [60 %],
    [supporting frit containing #ch("Pb") and #ch("Cd")], [21 %],
    [addition frit lead free], [7 %],
    [cadmium carbonate], [2 %],
    [kaolin], [4 %],
    [ball clay], [6 %],
    table.hline(stroke: 0.5pt),
    [], [100 %]
  ))
]

It was clear that one had to create certain conditions with regard to the compositions of the yellow and red glaze frits to realize a good colour development and to avoid all kinds of ceramic defects such as crater-forming, boiling, burning-away of the colour, etc.

The fact that compositions of yellow and red glazes are restricted in their compositions is also illustrated by the following fact. @tab:analyses-cadmium-yellow shows analyses made in 1972#super[@lit:report-chemical-laboratory] of four cadmium yellow glazes of four different manufacturers. As can be seen, the compositions do not deviate that much. All four glazes are in general comparable after firing although they differ on minor points.

The fact that frit and glaze compositions often have a preferred composition for the best possible development of a given colour is well known in ceramics. This is certain so in case of cadmium yellow and red frits and glazes. What the analyses, however, does not tell is how the different glazes have been manufactured. For sure, each of the glazes is built up from various frits. How these different frit compositions differ and, even more important, how these frits have been manufactured cannot be seen from the analyses neither the milling formulae of the glazes.

