The development for a more stable glaze formulation was soon focused on the splitting up of the basic frits. From many trials it became clear that the optimum melting conditions for one component (#ch("S")) worked out very negative for the other component (#ch("Cd")). Investigations at the various frits learned that the loss of cadmium can easily go up to 80 % due to volatilization during the melting of the frit.#super[@lit:report-r2915-chemical] In case the melting conditions were not optimal the cadmium oxide could rather easily be reduced to cadmium metal which is even more volatile than cadmium oxide.#super[@lit:f-ullmann-enzyclopdie] Also the melting temperature had a dramatic effect on the retaining of the cadmium in the frit. Investigations clearly showed that bringing down the melting temperature from 1450 °C to 1350 °C while maintaining the same yield resulted in a 50 % less loss of cadmium oxide in the molten frit. The combination of these two facts and information as e.g. from the German patent 2 023 710 from Hercules Inc. was the start for a study for the development of a new generation of base frits for cadmium yellow and red glazes. The start was made by splitting up the basic (#ch("Cd + S")) frit for yellow glazes in a frit containing the cadmium and another part containing the sulphur and in such a way that the two frits together matched the same original basic frit again. Next step was to melt the splitted frits under three different melting conditions, viz. oxidizing, neutral and reducing melting conditions. See also @tab:melting-conditions. For the oxidizing conditions the choice was made in the end for the introduction of 2 % sodium nitrate in the raw batch composition. For the reducing conditions a choice was made for melting with less air and with a reducing agent in the batch composition. After testing various possibilities carbon (#ch("C")) was taken as the reducing agent. Also sugar was tried but was less effective. #ch("SiC") was also tested with good reducing properties but the disadvantage was that it causes contamination of the frit and later contamination of glazes in the mills resulting in pinholes.

#figure(
  table(
    columns: 2,
    stroke: none,
    align: (left, left),
    table.hline(stroke: 0.5pt),
    table.header([*Cd containing frit*], [*S containing frit*]),
    table.hline(stroke: 0.5pt),

    [Melting conditions], [Melting conditions],
    [(i) oxidizing], [(iv) oxidizing],
    [(ii) neutral], [(v) neutral],
    [(iii) reducing], [(vi) reducing],
  ),
  caption: [Experimental melting conditions for cadmium and sulphur-containing frits]
) <tab:melting-conditions>

Testing of the application of carbon as reducing agent learned that a quantity of 2 % was the optimum; 1 % was less effective and more than 2 % didn't render better results. These six frits (see @tab:melting-conditions) were tested again in the standard glaze composition by making combinations of resp. the oxidizing melted #ch("Cd") frit (i) with the three different molten #ch("S") frits (iv, v and vi); the neutral melted #ch("Cd") frit (ii) with the three different molten #ch("S") frits (iv, v and vi) and the reduced melted #ch("Cd") frit (iii) with the three different molten #ch("S") frits (iv, v and vi). The results clearly confirmed that the cadmium frit needed different smelting conditions as the sulphur containing frit. By combining the cadmium frit melted under oxidizing conditions (i) and the sulphur containing frit melted under reducing conditions (vi), the final results were significant better compared to the original yellow glaze formula.

Next step was to realize a really low-melting frit for the cadmium component as this had shown that it was the best way to retain as much as possible cadmium in the frit. Normally, an addition of the cadmium frit in the glaze composition was rather high to have a contribution of about 2 till 3 % of cadmium oxide through the frit. As the influence of a high amount of frit with low melting compositions should be too big in the glaze compositions, the choice was made for a cadmium content as high as possible in the low melting frit compositions. Two different low melting compositions were chosen as starting point, viz. a lead silicate and a sodium boron silicate.

#recipe[
  In the lead silicate the one mol #ch("PbO") was partly replaced by #ch("CdO"):

  #composition(
    [(a)], [#ch("PbO")], [0.50], [], [], [], [], [#ch("SiO2")], [2.00],
    [], [#ch("CdO")], [0.50], [], [], [], [], [], []
  )

  and in the sodium boron silicate the sodium was partly replaced by #ch("CdO"):

  #composition(
    [(b)], [#ch("Na2O")], [0.50], [], [#ch("B2O3")], [0.25], [], [#ch("SiO2")], [2.50],
    [], [#ch("CdO")], [0.50], [], [], [], [], [], []
  )
]

These two frits were tested in combination with the same S-containing frit (vi) to control its effectiveness. From all trials it appeared very clearly that the cadmium frit based on the lead silicate gave a good yellow colour but only till rather low firing temperatures (1040 °C -- 1060 °C). The cadmium frit based on the sodium boron silicate, however, showed good and strong yellow colour development also on higher firing temperatures. It was known that about 80 % of the cadmium was evaporating from the basic frits having both cadmium and sulphur in its composition. Therefore, the amount of cadmium in the new trial frits was investigated. The theoretical amount of #ch("CdO") in the batch formula of the frit (b) is 24.38 %. After melting the amount was 24.02 % which means a loss of only 2 %. A tremendous improvement was realised with regard to retaining of the cadmium in the frit. One of the reasons was that a choice was made for easy and quick melting raw materials as boric acid and hydrated borax. The idea behind it was that these components start to melt very early. When boric acid is heated it gradually looses water until anhydrous boric acid (#ch("B2O3")) is formed.#super[@lit:j-r-taylor] This boric oxide melts at about 325 °C. Borax (#ch("Na2B4O7 * 10H2O")), on heating, begins to melt at 62 °C with frothing and five molecules of the crystallisation water have been lost at 100 °C. Four more molecules are lost at 160 °C until it is completely dehydrated at 400 °C. In fritting processes borax pentahydrate (#ch("Na2B4O7 * 5H2O")) is also often used. Dehydrated borax starts melting at 741 °C and is therefore a preferred material to starts the melting process during fritting. The early start of melting of boric compounds takes care that the cadmium oxide is already enwrapped by the molten boron components before going into solution and cannot evaporate that easy.

The following step was trying to improve the sulphur containing frit. By varying the amount of sulphur and also the source of it, it became clear that an amount of 1 % was the optimum. With 2 % sulphur or more a lot of bubbles appeared in the fired glaze which was even worse with 3 %. The introduction of #ch("S") through zinc sulphide (#ch("ZnS")) was shown the best one regarding the retaining and the stability of the frit.

#recipe[
  Variations of the components learned following:

  #table(
    columns: (auto, auto),
    stroke: none,
    [aluminium oxide:], [makes the compositions too hard at rather low percentage (4 %).],
    [soda:], [is not harmful for the yellow colour development very smooth surfaces can be realised, however, the influence on the coefficient of expansion becomes too big.],
    [borax:], [an amount between 5 and 15 % is not disadvantageous for the yellow colour. A bigger amount increases the forming of the vein structure of the glaze.],
    [quartz:], [not disadvantageous.],
    [zinc oxide:], [if more than 10 % is put into the formulae it has a clearly negative influence on the yellow colour development.],
    [barium carbonate:], [at quantities varying between 0 and 20 % no negative influence has been noticed.],
    [zinc sulphide:], [is needed for the necessary amount of sulphur.],
    [fluorspar:], [negative effect: forming of matt spots and craters.],
    [calcium carbonate:], [not negative for the yellow colour. Bigger amount makes the glaze too hard.],
    [strontium carbonate:], [same as for calcium carbonate.],
    [carbon / norit:], [is necessary as reducing agent.],
  )
]

All these testing of different raw materials finally resulted in a stable frit with a strong yellow colour development. Lithium was also tested, replacing part of the sodium content. It resulted in a very smooth surface at temperatures of 980 °C -- 1020 °C but the strength of the yellow colour became less, especially at the sharp rims of darker coloured bodies and at higher temperatures (1080 °C -- 1120 °C).

#recipe[
  All the investigations resulted in the following basic sulphur containing frit:

  #composition(
    [], [#ch("Na2O")], [0.467], [], [#ch("Al2O3")], [0.129], [], [#ch("SiO2")], [2.346],
    [], [#ch("K2O")], [0.052], [], [#ch("B2O3")], [0.112], [], [#ch("S")], [0.077],
    [], [#ch("CaO")], [0.173], [], [], [], [], [], [],
    [], [#ch("ZnO")], [0.308], [], [], [], [], [], [],
  )
]

This sulphur containing frit melted at about 1350 °C under reducing conditions (meaning with less air than normal and including a reducing agent in the batch formula), together with the cadmium containing frit melted at 1250 °C under oxidizing conditions was the base for a new strong cadmium yellow melted-in glaze. Its colour strength was compared to the standard cadmium yellow glazes by mixing 50 parts of the yellow glazes with 50 parts of a standard cadmium red glaze. With this test it was clear that the new frit combination gave a much more yellow-orange colour compared to the 'old' frits which was an indication that the new yellow glaze had more colour strength compared to the old glaze composition.

Now having the best possible cadmium and sulphur base frits, the next step was trying to find the best possible glaze composition which should fit the requirements as covering power and colour strength also on dark manganese bodies, covering power on sharp rims of cups and saucers, firing stability on high firing temperatures as high as 1200 °C, etc. Various addition frits were tested among them high and low lead containing frits, very stable and proven unleaded alkali-calcium frits, high barium containing frits, zinc containing frits, etc. and finally the best composition was found:

#recipe[
  #align(center, table(
    columns: 2,
    stroke: none,
    align: (left, right),
    [basic sulphur containing frit], [48 parts],
    [basic cadmium containing frit], [7 parts],
    [transparent u/l addition frit], [32 parts],
    [sodium boron silicate], [8 parts],
    [ball clay], [5 parts],
    table.hline(stroke: 0.5pt),
    [], [100 parts]
  ))
]

This glaze was tested at customers under various firing conditions and production conditions, on various bodies (white, red and manganese brown burning), at both wall- and floor tiles and at vases and dinnerware and showed good colour strength even at 1200 °C. One of the best and very temperature stable cadmium yellow glazes with high colour strength was born.#super[@lit:lw-baaij-ontwikkeling] This glaze is still promoted and in the market as can be seen in Ferro's Technical Information GL07.

As can be noticed no addition of cadmium carbonate was necessary anymore to stabilize the yellow colour. This was an improvement regarding the health and safety regulations besides the economical advantage. The glaze powder was tested according to the stomach acid simulation test (#ch("1/4N HCl") for 1 hour) and the lead- and cadmium release was found below the limits set. Also a lead- and cadmium release test with 4 % acetic acid for 24 hours (DIN EN 1388-1/-2) showed a release below the limits as set for this test.

The investigations continued with the aim to improve the existing red cadmium selenium glazes in the same way as the yellow glazes. Till the end of the 60ties -- early 70ties the basic frits contained cadmium as well as sulphur and selenium. Also here, a split was made in the way that the necessary amount of cadmium was introduced through the new developed and low melting and concentrated cadmium frit. The sulphur was in most cases introduced already through zinc sulphide. The necessary amount of selenium was introduced through an alkaline selenite, mostly sodium selenite (#ch("Na2SeO3")) or zinc selenite (#ch("ZnSeO3")).

Now a switch is made to the manufacturing of #ch("CdSSe") red colour stains as the development made in that area had later on a great impact on the cadmium-selenium frit and glaze development. Till the end of the 19th century #ch("CdSSe") stains were made in a dry way by mixing the materials cadmium sulphide, sulphur and metallic selenium together and heating the mix in a, sometimes rotating, kiln at about 600 °C.

Different manufacturers may have applied somewhat different combinations of raw materials and firing conditions but this was the general basis.

It doesn't ask that much imaginative faculty to realise that this method was very much unhealthy and uneconomical due to the volatibility of the selenium metal and also the cadmium compounds. This method was step by step replaced by the wet-chemical precipitation method which was invented by BASF in 1919. By this process the #ch("Se") metal was dissolved in #ch("Na2S"). This solution was added to a cadmium salt solution. A precipitate of cadmium-sulpho-selenide (#ch("CdSSe")) was the result and this precipitate was washed and dried at the proper temperature. By changing the amounts of resp. cadmium, sulphide and selenium, but also with additions of e.g., zinc oxide or aluminium oxide, one was able to influence the colour tone of the pigment. This wet method replaced the dry method more and more during the 20th century.

Following this process for the wet preparation of #ch("CdSSe") stains, a method was found in the early 70ties for a better introduction of these components for the selenium frit production. This method was very similar to the method of the wet preparation of #ch("CdSSe") stains. Basically, the cadmium was replaced by zinc and the following procedure was followed:

#narrow[
- zinc sulphate was dissolved in water of 50 °C and pumped into a reaction vessel
- sodium carbonate was also dissolved in water of 50 °C and also pumped into the reaction vessel
- sodium sulphide was dissolved in water of 45 °C and a quantity of selenium metal was added, stirred till it was dissolved
- the sodium sulphide + selenium solution was pumped into the reaction vessel resulting in a precipitate
- washing of the precipitate and centrifuging of the washed material
- drying of the material.
]

#recipe[
  The reactions which took place were following:

  #table(
    columns: (10%, auto),
    stroke: none,
    align: (left, left),

    [(i)], [
      #ch("ZnSO4") solution + #ch("Na2CO3") solution \
      #ch("3H2O + 5Zn^2+ + 5CO3^2- -> Zn5(CO3)2(OH)6 v + 3CO2 ^")
    ],

    [], [],

    [(ii)], [
      #ch("Na2S") + #ch("Se") solution \
      #ch("Na2S + Se -> 2Na+ + SSe^2-")
    ],

    [], [],

    [(iii)], [
      (i) + (ii) \
      #ch("Zn5(CO3)2(OH)6 v + 3SSe^2- -> Zn5(CO3)2(SSe)3 v + 6OH-")
    ]
  )
]

The resulting precipitate of zinc-sulpho-selenide (#ch("ZnSSe")) was centrifuged, dried and later on mixed with quartz in a ratio 1 : 3 for better handling and weighing of the precipitate.

#recipe[
  The precipitate of zinc-sulpho-selenide had the following typical composition:

  #align(center, table(
